1
00:00:01,230 --> 00:00:03,330
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,490 --> 00:00:03,910
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,430 --> 00:00:10,190
My name is Catherine and today we're talking about American education and college life.

4
00:00:10,590 --> 00:00:10,890
That's right.

5
00:00:11,050 --> 00:00:19,250
This is actually a user suggestion that they wanted to know more or less what it would be like a dialogue between friends on a typical day in college.

6
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:21,760
Alright so this is an advanced level lesson.

7
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:24,480
We're going to be talking in a normal conversational speed.

8
00:00:24,770 --> 00:00:30,350
We're going to listen to the dialogue and when we come back we'll talk about some of these key phrases and words that might help you understand the dialogue better.

9
00:00:36,850 --> 00:00:37,990
Hey Jordan, is that you?

10
00:00:38,190 --> 00:00:38,930
Long time no see.

11
00:00:39,550 --> 00:00:40,470
Oh hey, no kidding.

12
00:00:41,030 --> 00:00:43,510
I haven't seen you since orientation three months ago.

13
00:00:44,440 --> 00:00:45,280
So how you been?

14
00:00:45,960 --> 00:00:47,120
Settling into college life okay?

15
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:48,500
Yeah, I think so.

16
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:52,560
I pledged Phi Iota Alpha so I'm living at the frat house now.

17
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,480
Oh, so you're a frat boy now, huh?

18
00:00:55,680 --> 00:00:56,760
Yeah, yeah, I know.

19
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,560
That's totally cliche, but really I think it's been a good decision.

20
00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:02,740
Got a lot of support and good suggestions from the guys.

21
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:03,940
What about you?

22
00:01:04,560 --> 00:01:05,040
What have you been up to?

23
00:01:05,560 --> 00:01:06,220
Well, not much.

24
00:01:07,040 --> 00:01:09,480
I'm still living at home and commuting to school.

25
00:01:10,140 --> 00:01:13,680
I ended up dropping that metalworking class I was so excited about.

26
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:16,260
It just wasn't as interesting as I'd hoped.

27
00:01:16,920 --> 00:01:22,440
The guidance counselor suggested that I focus on my prerequisite courses so that I can make sure the credits count.

28
00:01:22,960 --> 00:01:25,420
That sounds smart, but kind of boring.

29
00:01:25,960 --> 00:01:27,500
Yeah, it is a little bit.

30
00:01:28,260 --> 00:01:31,480
I joined the Great Outdoors Club though, which has been a lot of fun.

31
00:01:31,960 --> 00:01:35,460
We've gone on two camping trips already and I've made some good friends.

32
00:01:35,940 --> 00:01:36,460
That's cool.

33
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:39,000
Hey, so have you decided on your major yet?

34
00:01:39,760 --> 00:01:40,760
Definitely pre-med.

35
00:01:40,760 --> 00:01:41,120
What about you?

36
00:01:41,760 --> 00:01:47,440
I still have no clue, but we don't have to declare a major until our sophomore year, so I've got time.

37
00:01:47,980 --> 00:01:48,940
Oops, I'm late for class.

38
00:01:49,240 --> 00:01:50,240
I've got to run.

39
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:50,800
Okay, take care.

40
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:52,540
Hey, nice running into you.

41
00:01:52,880 --> 00:01:53,400
Yeah, you too.

42
00:01:57,560 --> 00:01:58,560
All right, we're back.

43
00:01:58,560 --> 00:02:04,320
So now let's start with all these words and phrases on Language Takeaway.

44
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:04,600
Language Takeaway.

45
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:08,410
So hey Jordan, is that you?

46
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:09,810
Long time no see.

47
00:02:10,310 --> 00:02:10,930
I love this phrase.

48
00:02:10,970 --> 00:02:16,570
Right, this phrase, long time no see, which if you look at it, it doesn't really make sense.

49
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:17,950
It looks like we're missing a couple of words.

50
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:19,510
It looks like an incomplete sentence.

51
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:22,600
Yeah, like the subject, who?

52
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:23,600
Right.

53
00:02:23,650 --> 00:02:25,690
I haven't seen you, you haven't seen you.

54
00:02:26,030 --> 00:02:30,730
So really the meaning of this is I haven't seen you in a long time.

55
00:02:31,230 --> 00:02:31,510
That's right.

56
00:02:32,440 --> 00:02:36,680
And actually I've heard that this phrase was taken from Chinese.

57
00:02:36,680 --> 00:02:39,130
I've also heard that, so it's kind of like pigeon English.

58
00:02:39,630 --> 00:02:42,030
And now it's very, very common in English to say this.

59
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:43,200
You say this with your friends.

60
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:43,790
It's very casual.

61
00:02:44,590 --> 00:02:46,190
And yeah, long time no see.

62
00:02:46,520 --> 00:02:50,800
Because if you translate this literally to Chinese, it makes sense, right?

63
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:51,800
Yes, it does.

64
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:52,630
So it's interesting.

65
00:02:53,370 --> 00:02:55,590
All right, so we haven't seen our friend in a long time.

66
00:02:56,230 --> 00:02:57,470
And he's like, yeah, no kidding.

67
00:02:57,630 --> 00:02:57,930
You know what?

68
00:02:58,170 --> 00:02:59,870
I think it's been since orientation.

69
00:03:00,560 --> 00:03:03,160
So what is this orientation that he's talking about?

70
00:03:03,160 --> 00:03:06,980
Now this is an important part of the average American college experience.

71
00:03:08,160 --> 00:03:14,780
Orientation is a special time, maybe for one week or a couple of days, at the very beginning of your first year of university.

72
00:03:15,700 --> 00:03:25,520
All right, so my first week of school, I didn't have any classes, but I went to school and with all of the other first year students, we're called freshmen, I had to do activities and meet people and sign up for classes.

73
00:03:25,960 --> 00:03:31,080
And basically, orientation is a time when you learn about a new system or a new program.

74
00:03:31,080 --> 00:03:43,100
So how it works, because maybe you don't really know yet how to pick your classes or where the dorms are or how the meals work if you have to go to the cafeteria, etc., right?

75
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:44,840
Exactly.

76
00:03:44,840 --> 00:03:45,560
And it comes from the verb to orient.

77
00:03:46,160 --> 00:03:48,980
So I orient myself by facing the right direction.

78
00:03:49,520 --> 00:03:52,920
So orientation is really about teaching you how to start in a new system.

79
00:03:53,300 --> 00:03:54,120
Okay, very good.

80
00:03:54,680 --> 00:03:58,880
So well, that was three months ago and a lot has happened.

81
00:03:58,880 --> 00:04:02,500
Our speaker says that he pledged Phi Iota Alpha.

82
00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:05,680
So what is this to pledge in this?

83
00:04:05,680 --> 00:04:06,960
Now this is Greek as well.

84
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:11,560
Phi Iota Alpha is Greek and this means that we're talking about a fraternity.

85
00:04:12,360 --> 00:04:22,660
So fraternity is a group of boys, a sorority is a group of girls, and these are communities, so social societies that you have to apply to be a member of.

86
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:23,880
But we don't say apply.

87
00:04:24,480 --> 00:04:25,320
Right, you pledge.

88
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:27,900
So it's like I pledge allegiance.

89
00:04:28,320 --> 00:04:35,480
You say I want to be in your group, I want to be in your fraternity, and so you will have to do special things to become a member.

90
00:04:35,760 --> 00:04:43,640
So the verb to pledge would be to basically to promise something honorably or faithfully, right?

91
00:04:43,880 --> 00:04:44,240
Exactly.

92
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:48,080
And that's the nice way of putting it.

93
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:57,370
But generally when you talk about sororities and fraternities, these upperclassmen, so third, fourth year students, they make the students who are pledging do very weird things.

94
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:01,770
So as you said, this is for these two guys, this is for a fraternity.

95
00:05:02,860 --> 00:05:06,490
But if it's this type of club for girls, it's called a?

96
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:07,410
Sorority.

97
00:05:07,960 --> 00:05:08,960
Okay, so it's different.

98
00:05:08,960 --> 00:05:13,930
So I'm pledging this sorority, that means I'm trying to become a member of this sorority.

99
00:05:14,480 --> 00:05:23,960
So I think it's worthwhile mentioning many of you have probably seen it in movies, these fraternities or sororities, and they all have names based on the Greek alphabet.

100
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:34,840
Right, so the letters, Phi, Iota, Alpha, or Kappa, Kappa, Gamma, these are the letters that basically represent the name of the fraternity.

101
00:05:34,840 --> 00:05:43,760
And what's interesting is that maybe I am in Kappa Kappa Gamma at my school, there's another school with another Kappa Kappa Gamma, we're all related.

102
00:05:43,760 --> 00:05:46,520
It's the same social community.

103
00:05:46,520 --> 00:05:49,560
So they sometimes do events together, things like that.

104
00:05:49,560 --> 00:05:59,440
So actually these fraternities or sororities, it's not independent to a school, it's actually like a wide network of people that belong to this club.

105
00:05:59,440 --> 00:06:01,190
Exactly, it's a national network usually.

106
00:06:01,430 --> 00:06:02,650
So it's kind of like being in the Boy Scouts.

107
00:06:03,170 --> 00:06:05,730
Yeah, similar except the activities are much different.

108
00:06:06,390 --> 00:06:07,050
That's right.

109
00:06:07,490 --> 00:06:14,670
So these people, they may live at the frat house, like in this dialogue, he's living at the frat house.

110
00:06:14,990 --> 00:06:19,250
Now remember here, fraternity, that becomes frat if we're talking colloquially.

111
00:06:19,550 --> 00:06:25,890
So the frat house is the fraternity house, it's where those boys live, but only the Phi, Iota, Alpha boys.

112
00:06:26,210 --> 00:06:32,150
Right, so no one, if I have a friend and he's not Phi, Iota, Alpha, then he can't live there.

113
00:06:32,250 --> 00:06:35,730
Exactly, and we have a similar thing for girls, that's the sorority house.

114
00:06:36,210 --> 00:06:37,050
Okay, very good.

115
00:06:37,470 --> 00:06:41,390
So a frat house is the fraternity house, basically like their club house.

116
00:06:41,810 --> 00:06:42,490
Where they live though, yeah.

117
00:06:43,560 --> 00:06:49,520
And so sometimes guys that belong to fraternities, they're referred to as frat boys.

118
00:06:49,620 --> 00:07:04,740
Hey, frat boy, yeah, so fraternity boy, this is not always the nicest thing to say, it's got a negative meaning, because a lot of the activities that frat boys do stereotypically involve lots of drinking and maybe being loud.

119
00:07:05,640 --> 00:07:12,340
So frat boy is someone who's in a fraternity and it's usually a critical way of talking about those guys.

120
00:07:12,340 --> 00:07:18,340
So this whole fraternity, sorority thing, as he said, is a little bit cliche, right?

121
00:07:18,990 --> 00:07:21,590
Yeah, this is a great word, I use this a lot actually.

122
00:07:21,800 --> 00:07:30,120
So something that's cliche would be almost what's expected, but in a bad way, right?

123
00:07:30,120 --> 00:07:31,120
And so...

124
00:07:31,120 --> 00:07:34,800
So you joining a fraternity is a little bit cliche, because it's kind of like...

125
00:07:34,850 --> 00:07:39,850
It's what everyone does in the movies and on TV, like, oh, I'm going to college, I'm going to join a fraternity.

126
00:07:41,070 --> 00:07:43,790
It's almost like boring, it's so normal.

127
00:07:43,800 --> 00:07:52,960
But you could also say that like that movie, at the end of the movie, the man and the woman, they fought, but then they got back together, it's so cliche.

128
00:07:52,960 --> 00:07:56,450
So it's something that's overused, expected, very typical.

129
00:07:56,840 --> 00:07:57,110
Uninteresting.

130
00:07:57,840 --> 00:07:58,900
Uninteresting, okay.

131
00:07:58,900 --> 00:08:02,270
So we have a cliche, we're saying that a fraternity is cliche.

132
00:08:02,960 --> 00:08:09,090
Now, the other guy on the other hand is living off campus, he lives at home, but he said he commutes to school every day.

133
00:08:09,240 --> 00:08:11,070
So he says, I'm commuting to school.

134
00:08:12,120 --> 00:08:12,430
This is interesting.

135
00:08:13,120 --> 00:08:15,560
Now in America, there's usually two options.

136
00:08:15,560 --> 00:08:21,790
One is to live on campus, that means to live at your university in a dormitory or an apartment.

137
00:08:22,720 --> 00:08:28,430
But many people commute, and this means they drive to school every day, and then they drive home at night, they don't live on the campus.

138
00:08:29,070 --> 00:08:31,690
So you can also commute to work every day, right?

139
00:08:31,870 --> 00:08:33,470
This is what people do, you don't live at the office.

140
00:08:33,760 --> 00:08:33,970
Exactly.

141
00:08:34,270 --> 00:08:34,670
I hope not.

142
00:08:35,760 --> 00:08:40,610
No, this is a common thing, people will drive to work, and you might ask them, how long is your commute?

143
00:08:40,920 --> 00:08:44,160
So you say, oh, one hour, that means I drive one hour to work.

144
00:08:44,160 --> 00:08:48,480
So if I take the bus or the train, the subway, is that also commuting?

145
00:08:48,480 --> 00:08:56,500
It is, it doesn't matter how you do it, it just means you don't live where you work or where you study, so you have to travel there by some means of transportation.

146
00:08:56,780 --> 00:08:57,100
Okay.

147
00:08:57,820 --> 00:09:06,720
So, well, now going back to the academic aspect of this dialogue, he said that he ended up dropping a class.

148
00:09:07,540 --> 00:09:12,440
Okay, so to drop means to stop attending a class.

149
00:09:13,180 --> 00:09:23,080
Maybe you don't have enough time, maybe you don't like it, but to drop a class means you will no longer study, you will not take the tests, you will not get a grade.

150
00:09:23,700 --> 00:09:28,780
It's the opposite of to sign up for a class or to register for a class.

151
00:09:29,120 --> 00:09:37,880
So the difference between dropping a class and failing a class is that basically you're making the decision to not go there anymore.

152
00:09:38,220 --> 00:09:38,620
Exactly.

153
00:09:39,520 --> 00:09:48,400
So usually there's a time you can do this, so for the first two weeks you can attend classes and if you don't have enough time or you don't like a class, you can drop the class.

154
00:09:48,400 --> 00:09:53,940
But if you stay in the class, you just don't come or you do very poorly on your tests, that's called failing.

155
00:09:54,640 --> 00:10:02,000
So there are some classes, because I don't know, can you drop any class or do you have to take them at some point?

156
00:10:02,440 --> 00:10:02,780
That's a good question.

157
00:10:03,440 --> 00:10:06,320
That's our next phrase, that's a prerequisite course, right?

158
00:10:06,960 --> 00:10:16,940
So usually the university in America will tell you, you have to study math, you have to study one semester of science, you have to study English for one semester.

159
00:10:17,260 --> 00:10:18,680
So these are called prerequisites.

160
00:10:19,100 --> 00:10:22,180
It's when the university tells you, you have to study something.

161
00:10:23,220 --> 00:10:29,000
And I think you also have some courses that you can't take until you've passed some other course, right?

162
00:10:29,760 --> 00:10:35,480
So for example, you can't take economy unless you've passed calculus.

163
00:10:35,540 --> 00:10:35,960
Exactly.

164
00:10:36,480 --> 00:10:37,600
So that's another prerequisite.

165
00:10:37,620 --> 00:10:42,440
So we'd say, the prerequisite for economics is calculus one.

166
00:10:43,160 --> 00:10:49,800
Or the prerequisite for Spanish 200 is Spanish 100.

167
00:10:49,800 --> 00:10:51,980
Means you have to take something before you take something else.

168
00:10:52,440 --> 00:10:57,460
So there's two meanings of the word, but they're both related to requirements, what you have to do.

169
00:10:57,620 --> 00:10:58,940
Okay, you need a requirement.

170
00:10:59,840 --> 00:11:06,720
Now moving on and talking about the major, he said, well, if you decided on a major and he said, well, pre-med.

171
00:11:06,720 --> 00:11:11,200
All right, so major, first of all, is what you choose to study, right?

172
00:11:11,200 --> 00:11:12,840
The focus of your study.

173
00:11:12,840 --> 00:11:13,450
Exactly, the focus.

174
00:11:13,840 --> 00:11:19,360
You might study other classes, but your focus, your main interest area is one thing.

175
00:11:19,360 --> 00:11:21,840
And in his case, he's talking about pre-med.

176
00:11:21,840 --> 00:11:23,090
Now what does pre-med stand for?

177
00:11:23,250 --> 00:11:25,030
Because it is a short version of a longer word.

178
00:11:25,760 --> 00:11:26,760
So it's pre-medicine.

179
00:11:27,340 --> 00:11:27,860
Yeah, okay.

180
00:11:28,120 --> 00:11:32,640
So medicine is something you study if you want to be a doctor.

181
00:11:32,640 --> 00:11:36,880
Because in America, you don't become a doctor after four years.

182
00:11:36,880 --> 00:11:42,180
You have to go get your BA, your bachelor's degree, and then you have to get your PhD, your doctorate.

183
00:11:42,660 --> 00:11:48,360
So people who want to go to graduate school first have to study many required courses.

184
00:11:49,400 --> 00:11:51,760
That's the pre-med focus.

185
00:11:52,660 --> 00:11:54,120
Okay, so that's what he's talking about.

186
00:11:54,320 --> 00:11:56,980
He's definitely going to focus on pre-med.

187
00:11:56,980 --> 00:11:57,820
He wants to be a doctor.

188
00:11:58,160 --> 00:12:01,660
He's not going to be a doctor soon, but he wants to start studying to be a doctor.

189
00:12:02,020 --> 00:12:06,080
So he will become a pre-med, a pre-med major.

190
00:12:06,080 --> 00:12:08,880
And what about for lawyers?

191
00:12:08,880 --> 00:12:11,170
I know that there are also something that you focus on before.

192
00:12:11,880 --> 00:12:12,150
There is, yeah.

193
00:12:12,880 --> 00:12:13,880
That's called pre-law.

194
00:12:13,880 --> 00:12:14,880
Same idea.

195
00:12:14,880 --> 00:12:16,040
Pre means before.

196
00:12:16,040 --> 00:12:19,600
So obviously, a lawyer needs to study for a long time as well.

197
00:12:19,600 --> 00:12:24,120
And with your undergrad degree, so your first four years, you would study pre-law.

198
00:12:24,120 --> 00:12:30,620
So this is what you focus on for your four years, and then you go into grad school and become an attorney or a specific type of lawyer.

199
00:12:31,040 --> 00:12:32,660
But a lawyer doesn't have to study for a PhD.

200
00:12:33,440 --> 00:12:35,100
A lawyer studies for a JD.

201
00:12:35,940 --> 00:12:37,860
That's called a Juris Doctorate.

202
00:12:37,940 --> 00:12:38,660
That's the law degree.

203
00:12:39,180 --> 00:12:39,580
Interesting.

204
00:12:40,040 --> 00:12:41,040
Okay.

205
00:12:41,040 --> 00:12:45,660
So, well, he's going into pre-med, but the other guy is like, well, I don't have a clue yet.

206
00:12:46,640 --> 00:12:50,640
And he doesn't have to declare a major until the sophomore year.

207
00:12:51,970 --> 00:12:52,990
Okay, a couple of things going on here.

208
00:12:53,090 --> 00:12:56,160
First of all, there are different names for each year.

209
00:12:56,480 --> 00:12:59,480
So freshmen, sophomore, junior, senior.

210
00:12:59,480 --> 00:13:01,000
One, two, three, four.

211
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:02,420
So sophomore year is the second year.

212
00:13:02,620 --> 00:13:07,140
So if you say I'm a sophomore student at XY University, that means you're in your second year.

213
00:13:07,260 --> 00:13:07,500
Exactly.

214
00:13:09,160 --> 00:13:13,480
And he says that he doesn't have to declare a major until his second year, until his sophomore year.

215
00:13:13,480 --> 00:13:15,900
And this phrase, to declare a major, is very important.

216
00:13:16,760 --> 00:13:20,280
It means that you will tell the university what your focus will be.

217
00:13:20,640 --> 00:13:21,020
Right.

218
00:13:21,640 --> 00:13:21,840
And you have to do this.

219
00:13:22,640 --> 00:13:24,320
You can't just go to school and then graduate.

220
00:13:24,320 --> 00:13:30,560
You have to say, I will focus on this because every major has what we just talked about, prerequisites.

221
00:13:30,560 --> 00:13:31,560
Right.

222
00:13:31,560 --> 00:13:35,980
So, but in this first year, you don't have to tell the university yet what you want to study.

223
00:13:36,720 --> 00:13:41,200
Maybe you just, you take a variety of courses and then you tell them, okay, now I'm going to focus on this.

224
00:13:41,560 --> 00:13:42,560
Right.

225
00:13:42,560 --> 00:13:43,560
Exactly.

226
00:13:43,560 --> 00:13:44,560
That's what I did.

227
00:13:44,560 --> 00:13:46,560
And that's very common for liberal art schools.

228
00:13:46,560 --> 00:13:52,810
Maybe if you're in a specific trade school or you're in a certain track, you would have a different situation.

229
00:13:53,330 --> 00:13:58,550
But for me, liberal arts, I studied history, I studied philosophy, I studied language and literature.

230
00:13:59,400 --> 00:14:03,520
And then my sophomore year, my second year, I decided, oh, I will study philosophy.

231
00:14:03,950 --> 00:14:04,370
And that's.

232
00:14:04,520 --> 00:14:05,520
So you're going to focus on that.

233
00:14:05,520 --> 00:14:06,520
Yes.

234
00:14:06,520 --> 00:14:07,520
One thing.

235
00:14:07,520 --> 00:14:15,960
So it's, it's interesting because, uh, in many other countries, the education system requires you to declare your major before you start studying, right?

236
00:14:15,960 --> 00:14:17,450
You say, well, I'm going to study business.

237
00:14:17,450 --> 00:14:20,830
And then your, your four years are around that.

238
00:14:21,110 --> 00:14:26,250
Whereas in some, in some places in the United States or for some majors, you don't have to do that.

239
00:14:26,570 --> 00:14:26,990
That's true.

240
00:14:27,590 --> 00:14:32,710
In England, for example, you have one area of study, one major, and that's all you study, right?

241
00:14:33,520 --> 00:14:38,970
Because in, in, in the U S you can get a major in history with a minor in philosophy, right?

242
00:14:39,200 --> 00:14:39,350
Exactly.

243
00:14:40,200 --> 00:14:40,390
I was the opposite.

244
00:14:41,200 --> 00:14:44,170
I had a major in philosophy and a minor in history.

245
00:14:44,680 --> 00:14:48,390
So major means something bigger, more important and minor is smaller or less important.

246
00:14:48,480 --> 00:14:52,200
And a minor is, uh, is your second field of study.

247
00:14:52,200 --> 00:14:54,870
So you take fewer classes, but you still know something about that.

248
00:14:55,050 --> 00:15:02,680
But do they always have to be related because like, for example, can you be a major of philosophy with a minor in, I don't know, astronomy or something like that?

249
00:15:03,400 --> 00:15:04,400
Absolutely.

250
00:15:04,400 --> 00:15:05,400
Something like pre-med.

251
00:15:05,400 --> 00:15:08,880
So medicine, it's hard because there's so many requirements, right?

252
00:15:09,120 --> 00:15:15,600
But I had a number of friends who are major in computer science and a minor in, um, literature, for example.

253
00:15:15,600 --> 00:15:17,220
So you can, you can mix it up at certain schools.

254
00:15:17,900 --> 00:15:21,480
So what would be the advantage of that, uh, of doing something like that?

255
00:15:21,480 --> 00:15:31,280
You know, because most places or most education systems say that you or teach that, you know, you should specialize, you should be focused on one area.

256
00:15:31,280 --> 00:15:34,320
So if you're in computer science, then you should be focused on that.

257
00:15:34,320 --> 00:15:40,540
So why, why would somebody specialize in computer science in their major, but then take a minor in like literature or something?

258
00:15:40,790 --> 00:15:42,010
Well, let's use that example.

259
00:15:42,270 --> 00:15:49,770
I think one of the benefits for the liberal arts degree, and this is something they talk about a lot in education, is that you have a broad area of knowledge.

260
00:15:50,170 --> 00:15:52,030
So yes, you know a lot about computer science.

261
00:15:52,240 --> 00:16:02,960
You have to, to graduate, but maybe in your future you will write articles about computer science or you want a blog or you want a job that has a public relations aspect.

262
00:16:02,960 --> 00:16:11,340
You need to know how to write, and you're not going to learn how to write in all of your computer science classes, but you will by reading a lot and writing a lot in your literature classes.

263
00:16:11,340 --> 00:16:20,040
So some people like to say that it's very good to mix up business and languages or computer science and literature because it's a way to be well-rounded.

264
00:16:20,040 --> 00:16:21,040
That's right.

265
00:16:21,040 --> 00:16:31,040
That's actually interesting because in most places, specifically in the exact sciences fields, they focus more on that, like engineers.

266
00:16:31,520 --> 00:16:40,780
It's all about engineering, math, and all this, but the human aspect, like you said, or the liberal arts aspect, like literature or history, they don't get much of that.

267
00:16:40,980 --> 00:16:49,380
So when you actually have to become a manager or write or manage people, you don't really have the skills to do that because you never really took a class.

268
00:16:49,380 --> 00:16:54,910
That's right, and it can be very hard to learn how to write well and to speak well all of a sudden.

269
00:16:55,680 --> 00:16:56,680
Right, right.

270
00:16:56,680 --> 00:17:00,240
And I think that's where people struggle also when they're applying for jobs.

271
00:17:00,240 --> 00:17:01,010
So I don't know.

272
00:17:01,240 --> 00:17:03,030
I believe in being a well-rounded individual.

273
00:17:04,120 --> 00:17:08,930
Obviously there's some benefits to being very, very knowledgeable about one thing.

274
00:17:09,280 --> 00:17:19,520
